---
import SectionLayout from '../layouts/SectionLayout.astro';
import PlainEnglish from '../components/PlainEnglish.astro';
import LineChart from '../components/LineChart.astro';
import { withBase } from '../utils/url';

const priceCollapse = [
  { label: '2022', value: 25, display: '$25' },
  { label: '2023', value: 10, display: '$10' },
  { label: '2024', value: 3, display: '$3' },
  { label: '2025', value: 1.2, display: '$1.20' },
  { label: '2026', value: 0.5, display: '<$1' },
];
---

<SectionLayout
  slug="token-economics"
  kicker="Section 03"
  title="Token economics"
  dek="How inference is priced, why prices have collapsed 10x a year, and why that doesn't always mean cheaper AI."
>
  <PlainEnglish>
    <p>
      Every time you use an AI model, it's charged by the "word" (roughly) — tokens in (what you
      typed) and tokens out (what it wrote back). Writing new words is more expensive than reading
      old ones, so output costs more than input. Prices have crashed incredibly fast — the same
      quality of answer that cost $20-30 per million words in 2022 costs under $1 today — but
      because people also use AI so much more now, that doesn't always mean AI products are getting
      cheaper to run overall.
    </p>
  </PlainEnglish>

  <h2>The basic unit: input tokens vs. output tokens</h2>
  <p>Pricing is quoted per million tokens, split into two rates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Input tokens</strong> — everything sent to the model: your prompt, system instructions, chat history, retrieved documents, tool definitions. Cheaper, because the model can process all of it in parallel in one pass.</li>
    <li><strong>Output tokens</strong> — everything the model generates back. More expensive — often 4-8x the input price — because generation happens one token at a time, in sequence (each new token depends on the ones before it), which is inherently slower and more compute-intensive per token.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-note">
    <strong>The most-missed detail:</strong> because output costs several times more than input, a prompt that
    asks for a long, detailed answer can cost far more than a prompt of the same length asking for a short one —
    the input/output split matters more to your bill than total prompt length does.
  </div>

  <h2>Sample pricing <span class="section-note">(as of early 2026, illustrative — changes constantly)</span></h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>Input ($/M tokens)</th><th>Output ($/M tokens)</th><th>Output:input ratio</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Claude Opus (frontier)</td><td>~$5.00</td><td>~$25.00</td><td>5x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Claude Sonnet (mid-tier)</td><td>~$3.00</td><td>~$15.00</td><td>5x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GPT-5.2 (frontier)</td><td>~$1.75</td><td>~$14.00</td><td>8x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gemini Flash (budget)</td><td>~$0.10</td><td>~$0.40</td><td>4x</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Open-weight via inference clouds <span class="section-note">(Llama, DeepSeek, Qwen)</span></td><td>$0.05–$0.50</td><td>$0.20–$1.00</td><td>2-4x</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The spread between the cheapest and most expensive options for a comparable task can be 100x or more — model
    choice is one of the biggest cost levers a developer has. See how the different chips behind these prices
    compare in <a href={withBase('/gpu-models')}>Section 02: GPU models</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The price collapse — and why it happened</h2>
  <p>
    The cost of GPT-4-level performance has fallen roughly 10x per year since 2022 — from around $20-30 per
    million tokens to under $1 today:
  </p>
  <LineChart points={priceCollapse} logScale unit=" / M tokens" />
  <p>Five forces are driving it at once:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Cheaper compute per operation</strong> — each new GPU generation does more math per dollar.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantization</strong> — running models at lower numerical precision (16-bit → 8-bit → 4-bit) trades a small accuracy cost for a large speed/cost gain.</li>
    <li><strong>Smaller, more efficient models</strong> — architecture improvements let smaller models match older, larger ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Open-weight competition</strong> — free-to-download models (Llama, DeepSeek, Qwen) force every provider's prices down.</li>
    <li><strong>Software optimization</strong> — better serving techniques (batching, caching, speculative decoding) squeeze more throughput from the same hardware.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The margin math</h2>
  <p>
    The business logic behind an inference cloud or model API is simple in principle: gross margin = price
    charged minus cost to serve, as a share of price. If it costs a provider $1.00 per million tokens to run a
    model and they charge $2.00, that's a 50% gross margin; if their underlying cost drops to $0.40, the same
    $2.00 price yields an 80% margin — which is exactly why providers are racing to cut serving costs rather than
    just cutting prices.
  </p>

  <h2>The catch: headline price &ne; real cost</h2>
  <p>Production usage is rarely just "prompt in, answer out." Real costs stack on top of the headline per-token price:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reasoning/thinking tokens</strong> — some models generate hidden intermediate reasoning steps that are billed but never shown to the user.</li>
    <li><strong>Context and history</strong> — every follow-up message resends the prior conversation, so long chats cost more per turn, not less.</li>
    <li><strong>Tool calls and retries</strong> — agentic workflows that call tools, fail, and retry multiply token usage per completed task.</li>
    <li><strong>Caching</strong> — reusing a previously-processed prompt prefix is often billed at a discount, which rewards designing prompts for reuse.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This produces what some call the <strong>cost paradox</strong>: per-token prices have fallen 10x a year, but
    usage per product has grown even faster (sometimes 100x), so total inference spend for a fast-growing AI
    product can rise even as unit prices fall — this is the thread that leads directly into
    <a href={withBase('/financing')}>the financing section</a> next.
  </p>
</SectionLayout>

<style>
  .section-note {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.55em;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
</style>
